  'A board-and-batten Gothic Revival cottage of about 1852, one and a half stories, with its roof rafters left showing and a one-story porch on plain posts: Wikipedia calls it a distinctive example of mid-19th-century "pattern book" building. Listed on the National Register in 1985.',
